(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xxv-b"] = {"qs": [["Professionalism, Ethics & Compliance", "A sales staff member at an advisory firm is asked by a client to recommend a mutual fund scheme. The staff member should:", ["Recommend whichever scheme has the highest recent return", "Recommend a scheme from the staff member's personal portfolio", "Give advice as long as the client signs a waiver", "Direct the request to a qualified adviser"], 3, "Personal investment advice must come from qualified persons associated with investment advice. Non-core staff should route such requests to the adviser."], ["Securities Markets & Basics of Investment", "An investor wants to hold shares in electronic form. The account needed with a depository participant is a:", ["Savings bank account linked to a debit card", "Demat account", "Public Provident Fund account at a post office", "Trading account with a mutual fund distributor"], 1, "A demat account, opened with a DP of NSDL or CDSL, holds securities electronically. A trading account with a broker is used to place orders."], ["Professionalism, Ethics & Compliance", "Under SEBI's segregation requirement, an individual investment adviser:", ["May earn commissions from products recommended to advisory clients", "May act as both adviser and distributor if the client agrees orally", "Must earn all its income from distribution commissions", "Cannot provide distribution services to its advisory clients"], 3, "SEBI requires advisory and distribution to be segregated at the client level, so advice is not influenced by commissions. Advisory clients cannot be offered distribution services."], ["Personal Financial Planning & Financial Markets", "A client with high income, large savings and no debts, but who loses sleep over small market falls, has:", ["High risk capacity but low risk tolerance", "Low risk capacity and high risk tolerance", "High risk tolerance and high risk capacity", "No need for any risk profiling"], 0, "Risk capacity is the financial ability to bear losses, while risk tolerance is the emotional willingness. Advice should respect the lower of the two."], ["Financial Asset Classes", "Liquidity risk is highest for which asset?", ["A residential flat in a small town", "Units of a liquid mutual fund scheme", "Nifty 50 index ETF units", "A savings bank deposit"], 0, "Property can take months to sell and may require a price discount. Liquid funds, index ETFs and savings deposits can be converted to cash quickly."], ["Personal Financial Planning & Financial Markets", "Why is adequate term insurance usually addressed before investing in equity for a young earning parent?", ["Term insurance gives higher returns than equity over all periods", "It protects dependants if the earner dies prematurely", "Equity investments are prohibited until insurance is purchased", "Term insurance returns all premiums with a large bonus"], 1, "Protecting dependants' income is a foundation of planning. Pure term cover provides a large sum assured at low cost; it is protection, not an investment."], ["Financial Products", "A ULIP differs from a term insurance plan mainly because a ULIP:", ["Offers only a pure death benefit with no maturity value", "Guarantees a fixed maturity amount in all cases", "Combines insurance with market-linked investment", "Is regulated by SEBI rather than IRDAI"], 2, "ULIPs invest part of the premium in market-linked funds and provide some life cover. Term plans give pure protection. ULIPs are regulated by IRDAI."], ["Personal Financial Planning & Financial Markets", "Which regulator oversees insurance companies and insurance intermediaries in India?", ["IRDAI", "The Securities and Exchange Board of India through its investment adviser department", "The Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority", "The Reserve Bank of India through its banking ombudsman"], 0, "The Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India regulates insurers and insurance intermediaries. SEBI regulates securities markets, PFRDA regulates pensions and RBI regulates banks."], ["Securities Markets & Basics of Investment", "The present value of ₹1,10,000 receivable in one year, discounted at 10%, is:", ["₹99,000", "₹1,21,000", "₹1,00,000", "₹1,10,000"], 2, "Present value = 1,10,000 ÷ 1.10 = ₹1,00,000. Money received in the future is worth less today because it could be invested meanwhile."], ["Personal Financial Planning & Financial Markets", "A client's net worth is calculated as:", ["Total assets minus total liabilities", "Annual income minus annual taxes paid to the government", "Total assets plus the value of all outstanding loans", "Monthly savings multiplied by the number of working years left"], 0, "Net worth shows what the client would have if all assets were sold and all debts repaid. It is a key starting point for analysing the client's current financial position."], ["Sales Skills", "A client compares the adviser's fee with a 'free' online tip service. A fair response is to explain that:", ["Tip services are illegal and every user will face prosecution", "Advice is personalised, fiduciary and accountable", "The adviser guarantees returns, unlike the tip service", "The adviser's fee is negotiable to zero if the client insists"], 1, "An adviser's value lies in personalised, suitable advice with fiduciary duty and regulatory accountability. The adviser should avoid exaggerated claims or guarantees."], ["Financial Products", "An investor puts ₹25,000 into a scheme at NAV ₹62.50 (no stamp duty or loads). The units allotted are:", ["390.6", "400", "2,500", "40"], 1, "Units = amount ÷ NAV = 25,000 ÷ 62.50 = 400. Units allotted depend on the applicable NAV, which is determined by the time of receipt of funds."], ["Securities Markets & Basics of Investment", "A client's portfolio target is 50% equity and 50% debt. After a market fall, it is 40% equity and 60% debt. Rebalancing would mean:", ["Selling more equity to cut losses and move entirely to debt", "Leaving the portfolio unchanged indefinitely", "Buying equity and selling debt to restore 50:50", "Moving the whole portfolio into gold"], 2, "Rebalancing restores the target by buying the underweight asset and selling the overweight one. It enforces discipline and keeps risk at the intended level."], ["Sales Skills", "Active listening during client meetings involves:", ["Planning your next pitch while the client is speaking", "Interrupting to correct the client quickly", "Focusing only on figures and ignoring concerns", "Paraphrasing and clarifying what the client says"], 3, "Active listening means giving full attention, summarising the client's points and asking clarifying questions. It helps uncover real needs and builds trust."], ["Sales Skills", "A client insists on buying a stock tipped by a social media influencer. The best approach for sales staff is to:", ["Refer the request to the adviser for suitability review", "Immediately agree and help the client buy as much as possible", "Ridicule the client for following social media advice", "Recommend a more speculative stock instead"], 0, "Sales staff should not give their own recommendations. The adviser can assess suitability and explain risks, including unregistered persons' recommendations."], ["Financial Asset Classes", "A client compares a company's non-convertible debenture with its equity share. Which feature belongs to the debenture rather than the share?", ["A contractual right to interest and repayment", "Voting rights at the company's general meetings on major decisions", "A claim on residual profits after all other obligations are met", "Unlimited upside if the company's profits grow strongly"], 0, "Debenture holders are lenders with a contractual claim to interest and principal, ranking ahead of shareholders. Voting rights, residual profits and unlimited upside are features of equity ownership."], ["Sales Skills", "Which is an open-ended question useful in client profiling?", ["Do you want high returns?", "Are you a risk-taker?", "What would you like your money to achieve in ten years?", "Is ₹10 lakh your budget?"], 2, "Open-ended questions invite detailed answers that reveal goals and attitudes. Yes/no questions limit understanding and may lead to superficial profiling."], ["Financial Asset Classes", "Why do long-term government bonds fall more in price than short-term ones when interest rates rise?", ["They have higher credit risk than short-term bonds", "They have higher duration", "They pay no coupon at all", "They are not traded on any market"], 1, "Duration measures price sensitivity to rate changes, and longer bonds have higher duration. Government bonds carry little credit risk, but they still carry interest rate risk."], ["Professionalism, Ethics & Compliance", "Which body has SEBI recognised as the Investment Adviser Administration and Supervisory Body (IAASB)?", ["The Association of Mutual Funds in India", "The Reserve Bank of India", "The Institute of Chartered Accountants of India", "BSE Limited"], 3, "SEBI recognised BSE Limited, through a subsidiary, as IAASB to administer and supervise investment advisers. It handles enlistment, monitoring and complaint escalation."], ["Financial Products", "An ELSS mutual fund scheme is characterised by:", ["A guaranteed return over five years", "A three-year lock-in with equity exposure", "Investment only in government bonds", "No lock-in and daily redemption"], 1, "Equity Linked Savings Schemes invest mainly in equities and have a three-year lock-in. Tax deductions depend on the tax regime the investor chooses."], ["Professionalism, Ethics & Compliance", "An investment adviser must carry out risk profiling of clients mainly to:", ["Decide how much commission to earn", "Choose the client's tax regime", "Replace KYC verification", "Ensure that advice is suitable"], 3, "Risk profiling assesses capacity and tolerance so that recommendations match the client. It is separate from KYC and from any commission considerations, which IAs cannot earn."], ["Financial Asset Classes", "Treasury bills issued by the Government of India:", ["Pay a fixed coupon every six months for ten years", "Are equity instruments with voting rights", "Are issued at a discount and redeemed at face value", "Carry credit risk similar to unrated corporate bonds"], 2, "T-bills are short-term discount instruments with maturities up to 364 days. The difference between issue price and face value is the investor's return."], ["Professionalism, Ethics & Compliance", "An investment adviser's client agreement must cover:", ["Promise of a minimum annual return to the client", "The client's waiver of the right to complain", "The adviser's right to trade in the client's account at will", "Terms of services, fees and the adviser's obligations"], 3, "SEBI requires a client agreement with prescribed terms covering services, fees, conflicts, and obligations. Assured returns and waivers of client rights are not permitted."], ["Financial Asset Classes", "A buyer of a put option on a stock benefits when:", ["The stock price rises well above the strike price", "The stock price stays exactly at the purchase price", "The stock price falls below the strike price", "The company declares a large dividend in cash"], 2, "A put gives the right to sell at the strike price, so it gains value as the underlying price falls. The buyer's maximum loss is the premium paid."], ["Sales Skills", "A prospect says, 'I'll think about it.' The most appropriate response is to:", ["Tell him the fee will double if he does not sign today", "Offer to answer questions and agree on a follow-up", "Keep calling him several times a day until he agrees", "Tell him other clients have made guaranteed profits"], 1, "Respecting the client's decision time while staying available builds credibility. False urgency, harassment or claims of guaranteed profits are unethical."], ["Financial Products", "The direct plan of a mutual fund scheme has a lower expense ratio than the regular plan because:", ["It invests in a completely different portfolio of securities", "It does not pay distributor commission", "It is managed by a less experienced fund manager", "SEBI subsidises its costs from public funds"], 1, "Both plans share the same portfolio and manager. The regular plan's higher TER includes distribution commission, which the direct plan excludes."], ["Personal Financial Planning & Financial Markets", "In the financial planning process, the step after implementing recommendations is:", ["Monitoring and periodic review", "Collecting the client's KYC documents for the first time", "Setting the client's goals and objectives for the first time", "Closing the client relationship permanently"], 0, "Plans must be reviewed as markets, laws and the client's life change. Data gathering and goal setting come at the start of the process."], ["Professionalism, Ethics & Compliance", "Fees charged by an investment adviser to clients must be paid through:", ["Banking channels, such as cheques or online transfers", "Cash handed over at the client's home", "Gift cards and vouchers", "Cryptocurrency transfers"], 0, "Fees must be paid through traceable banking channels; cash is not permitted. SEBI's centralised fee collection mechanism further helps ensure payment only to registered advisers."], ["Securities Markets & Basics of Investment", "An investor earns 9% on a bond fund when inflation is 6%. The approximate real return is:", ["15%", "3%", "9%", "1.5%"], 1, "Real return ≈ nominal return − inflation = 9 − 6 = 3%. The exact figure (1.09 ÷ 1.06 − 1) is about 2.8%."], ["Financial Asset Classes", "Which risk most directly affects the holder of a corporate bond rated BBB compared with a AAA bond of similar maturity?", ["Higher currency risk since BBB bonds are always issued abroad", "Lower interest rate risk because of the lower rating", "Higher credit risk of default", "Zero liquidity risk because BBB bonds trade daily"], 2, "Lower-rated bonds have a higher chance of default, so they pay higher yields. Ratings do not determine currency or remove liquidity risk."], ["Financial Products", "Which product would generally suit a retired client who needs regular income with low risk?", ["Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme", "A small-cap equity fund with high volatility", "A Category III AIF using leverage", "An equity derivatives trading strategy"], 0, "SCSS is a government-backed scheme paying quarterly interest, suitable for retirees seeking safety and income. The other options carry high volatility."], ["Financial Products", "A client wants to invest ₹10,000 a month into an equity fund for 15 years. The most suitable mechanism is:", ["A Systematic Withdrawal Plan from an existing debt scheme", "A Systematic Investment Plan", "A one-time lump sum in a fixed maturity plan", "A five-year bank recurring deposit"], 1, "A SIP invests a fixed amount at regular intervals, building wealth gradually and averaging purchase costs over market cycles. An SWP does the opposite, withdrawing money."], ["Personal Financial Planning & Financial Markets", "A client earns ₹1.2 lakh a month and spends ₹80,000. A common guideline is to hold 6 months of expenses as an emergency fund. The target fund is:", ["₹7.2 lakh", "₹2.4 lakh", "₹6.0 lakh", "₹4.8 lakh"], 3, "Emergency funds are based on expenses, not income: 6 × ₹80,000 = ₹4.8 lakh. Using income would give ₹7.2 lakh, which overstates the need."], ["Financial Asset Classes", "Sovereign Gold Bonds offer investors:", ["Physical delivery of gold bars at maturity with no interest", "Guaranteed return of capital regardless of gold prices", "Gold price exposure plus fixed interest", "Exposure to gold mining company shares"], 2, "SGBs track the gold price and pay fixed annual interest on the issue value. Redemption is in cash at prevailing gold prices, so value can fall."], ["Securities Markets & Basics of Investment", "Asset allocation is considered the most important investment decision for most clients because it:", ["Guarantees that the portfolio will never fall in value", "Removes the need to review the portfolio ever again", "Ensures that the client beats every benchmark each year", "Largely determines a portfolio's overall risk and return"], 3, "How money is split among equity, debt, gold and cash drives most of the variability in portfolio returns. It does not guarantee outcomes or remove the need for reviews."], ["Professionalism, Ethics & Compliance", "An investment adviser using AI tools to generate advice must:", ["Avoid any disclosure, as AI use is a trade secret", "Pass responsibility for any errors to the AI provider", "Obtain the client's consent to waive suitability", "Disclose AI use and remain responsible for the advice"], 3, "SEBI's amended framework requires advisers to disclose AI use to clients and holds the adviser responsible for the advice, data security and compliance."], ["Financial Products", "The minimum investment in a Specialised Investment Fund (SIF) under SEBI's framework is:", ["₹500 through a monthly SIP", "₹1 crore, the same as AIFs", "₹10 lakh per investor across SIF strategies", "₹50 lakh, the same as PMS"], 2, "SEBI set a ₹10 lakh minimum per investor across all SIF strategies of an AMC (accredited investors excepted), positioning SIFs between mutual funds and PMS."], ["Sales Skills", "Documenting each client meeting in the CRM matters mainly because it:", ["Lets the sales team share client details on social media", "Replaces the need for a written advisory agreement", "Guarantees that the client will renew", "Supports continuity, compliance and suitability records"], 3, "Records of discussions, preferences and decisions support service continuity when staff change and demonstrate compliance with the adviser's suitability and record-keeping requirements."], ["Personal Financial Planning & Financial Markets", "Which regulator administers the National Pension System?", ["SEBI, through the stock exchanges and depositories", "PFRDA", "IRDAI, through the life insurance companies", "The Ministry of Corporate Affairs, through the RoC"], 1, "The Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority regulates NPS and pension fund managers. Other regulators oversee securities, insurance and companies."], ["Sales Skills", "Closing an advisory engagement ethically means:", ["Persuading the client to sign before reading the agreement", "Offering gifts to encourage a quick signature", "Hiding the fee structure until after the first invoice", "Confirming the client understands terms, fees and risks before agreeing"], 3, "Ethical closing ensures informed consent: the client knows the services, fees and risks. Pressure, inducements or hidden fees breach regulations and trust."], ["Professionalism, Ethics & Compliance", "A sales executive tells a client that a recommended portfolio 'cannot lose money'. This statement is:", ["Misleading and prohibited", "Acceptable if said only verbally", "Required to build client confidence", "Allowed for conservative portfolios"], 0, "Assuring returns or no loss misrepresents risk and breaches SEBI's code of conduct. All investments carry risk, and communications must be fair and balanced."], ["Financial Products", "The riskometer of a mutual fund scheme helps the investor to:", ["Understand the scheme's risk level", "Know the exact return the scheme will deliver", "See the fund manager's personal investments", "Compare the scheme's fee with PMS fees"], 0, "The riskometer classifies a scheme's risk on a six-level scale from Low to Very High based on portfolio characteristics, and is reviewed monthly. It does not predict returns."], ["Securities Markets & Basics of Investment", "₹2 lakh invested at 8% compounded annually will grow in 3 years to approximately:", ["₹2.48 lakh", "₹2.52 lakh", "₹2.16 lakh", "₹2.60 lakh"], 1, "Future value = 2 × (1.08)³ = 2 × 1.2597 ≈ ₹2.52 lakh. Simple interest would give only ₹2.48 lakh, because compounding earns interest on interest."], ["Securities Markets & Basics of Investment", "Diversification across asset classes helps mainly because:", ["It guarantees a positive return every single year", "It eliminates market risk across all assets", "Different assets often do not move together", "It ensures the highest possible returns"], 2, "Assets with low correlation can offset each other, smoothing overall returns. Diversification reduces but does not eliminate risk or guarantee returns."], ["Personal Financial Planning & Financial Markets", "A 30-year-old wants ₹50 lakh for his child's education in 15 years. In financial planning terms, this is best classified as a:", ["Short-term goal that should be funded through a savings account", "Liquidity need that must be met within the next six months", "Long-term goal", "Risk tolerance measure based on psychology"], 2, "Goals more than about seven to ten years away are long-term, which generally allows a greater share of growth assets. The horizon shapes the investment approach."], ["Securities Markets & Basics of Investment", "Under the Rule of 72, an investment growing at 12% a year will double in roughly:", ["12 years", "8 years", "6 years", "4 years"], 2, "Years to double ≈ 72 ÷ 12 = 6 years. The rule is a quick approximation useful for explaining compounding to clients."], ["Financial Asset Classes", "REITs are suitable for investors who want:", ["Guaranteed rental income fixed for 20 years", "Ownership of a specific flat in their own name", "An instrument with no market price risk", "Real estate exposure with smaller tickets and listing"], 3, "REITs pool income-producing commercial properties and list on exchanges, offering smaller-ticket, more liquid access. Distributions vary and unit prices fluctuate."], ["Sales Skills", "An effective first meeting with a prospective advisory client should focus mainly on:", ["Understanding the client's goals, situation and concerns", "Presenting the firm's best-performing recommendation of the past year", "Getting the client to sign the agreement before the meeting ends", "Explaining market forecasts for the next quarter in detail"], 0, "Advisory relationships begin with discovery. Understanding needs builds trust and is required for suitable advice; product pitches and pressure to sign come at the wrong stage."]]};
